<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS COMMENT SITS INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT DELIBERATELY. A comment placed ABOVE
        <CustomField> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with a misleading "unable to find
        matching parent xml file" error.

        DISPOSITION GAP CLOSURE TRANCHE 1, ITEM 3 (agent-output/disposition-gap-closure-t1-design.md
        §3 Item 3, premise P-6, decision D-2, both settled). Hand-entered, not required, no
        default. Date, not DateTime - per-day granularity is all this field is asked for.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 RELATIONSHIP TO Release_Materials_Response__c - THE REQUEST'S FRAMING WAS WRONG. THIS
        FIELD IS NOT A DUPLICATE OF THAT OBJECT. RECORD WHY, SO NOBODY TRIES TO MERGE THEM.
        =====================================================================================
        Release_Materials_Response__c carries Disposition__c, Broker__c, Entry_DateTime__c,
        Method__c, Notes__c. It has NO Buyer__c and NO NDA__c lookup. It is
        DISPOSITION-SCOPED and BROKER-SCOPED, so it structurally CANNOT record a per-buyer
        response at any price without a schema change, which is out of scope here.

            NDA__c.Buyer_Response_Date__c (this field)   Release_Materials_Response__c (existing)
            Scope: per NDA = per PARTY                   Scope: per disposition, per BROKER
            Names a buyer? YES - NDA carries Buyer__c     Names a buyer? NO - no Buyer__c/NDA__c
            and Party_Role__c = 'Buyer'                   lookup exists on the object at all
            Granularity: Date                             Granularity: DateTime + Method + Notes

        They are COMPLEMENTARY, not duplicates. This is the classic per-party join trap: a
        parent-scoped log cannot answer a child-scoped question, however similar the field names
        look.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 dispositionBuyerTimeline DOES NOT READ THIS FIELD IN T1 - DEFERRED, PER D-2.
        =====================================================================================
        T1 ships the FIELD + FLS only. Adding it to NdaSelector.selectBuyerTimelineByDispositionId
        would be a WITH USER_MODE selector widening = an FLS change, and
        DispositionBuyerTimelineController deliberately does NOT fail soft on that selector
        ("a QueryException is NOT swallowed into an empty list") - a missing grant would
        red-banner the whole timeline card for every user until reconciled. That read belongs
        with a future tranche that reworks the timeline, not this schema-and-access tranche.
        ⚠ If it IS read later: (a) any NDA-field read from a disposition persona must be
        grant-checked field by field, never assumed - the two disposition sets grant only FOUR
        NDA__c fields today and Date_Signed__c / Disposition__c / Counterparty_Name__c /
        NDA_Signed__c / Date_Sent__c are granted ONLY to the acquisition sets; (b) it must sit
        behind buildRow's early return for Declined rows, exactly as Date_Signed__c does, or a
        declined party's retained response date leaks onto a row the service is documented to
        keep date-free.

        FLS: applied in the consolidated permission-set pass, not this file. Risk: LOW as
        scoped (field + FLS only); escalates to MEDIUM only if a future tranche pulls the
        timeline read forward without repeating the checks above.
    -->
    <fullName>Buyer_Response_Date__c</fullName>
    <description>Date this NDA&apos;s buyer party responded (per-party signal, distinct from Release_Materials_Response__c which is disposition-and-broker-scoped, not per-buyer - see the XML comment in this file). Hand-entered. Not read by any tranche-1 selector or LWC.</description>
    <inlineHelpText>The date this buyer responded to the NDA. Distinct from the broker-level materials response log.</inlineHelpText>
    <label>Buyer Response Date</label>
    <required>false</required>
    <trackHistory>false</trackHistory>
    <trackTrending>false</trackTrending>
    <type>Date</type>
</CustomField>
